---
// Servco's "sole distributor since 1958" claim from the earlier draft turned out misleading:
// Big Island Toyota was independently owned by the DeLuz/Correira/Higashi families from 1962
// until Servco acquired it in December 2024 (Kyle's own father-in-law worked there), so "sole"
// wasn't accurate. Fixed by dropping that framing rather than replacing it with the dealership
// story, per Kyle's call that it's real but not what this section should center. The Jeep
// Wrangler "streak broken in 2018" stat was also dropped: real as a statistic, but the source
// itself attributes the 2018 spike to rental-fleet purchases, not local buyers, exactly the kind
// of sales-ranking noise Kyle wanted this section to move away from. Replaced with real,
// independently verified pop-culture and market material: Jo Koy's Maui bit (Hawaii News Now),
// Poi Pounder, a 1984-85 Toyota Pickup body built into Hawaii's first monster truck (debuted
// 1985, retired 1991), per the Monster Trucks Wiki, corroborated by a Toyotaholics Facebook
// post from someone who remembered it as "our monster truck," and confirmed visually: the
// wiki's own surviving photos unmistakably show a real Toyota Pickup cab under the rig, so this
// is written with confidence, not hedged, on the truck's identity, and the state's own pickup-
// segment and Tacoma-share numbers (Hawaii Auto Outlook, published by the Hawaii Automobile
// Dealers Association, Q1 2025, sourced from Experian Automotive), which also independently
// confirms the 27.6% overall Toyota market-share figure below (27.6% for full-year 2024, exact
// match). The original third card, a Kalapana bumper sticker, was Kyle's own call: real, but
// personal taste with no actual tie to Toyota or truck culture. The Kuha'o Zane/Tacoma design-
// collaboration card was also Kyle's call to cut.
// A card was added on Kyle's tip: High Watah, a band from Moloka'i, released "Sand In My Boots"
// in January 2025. First pass on this called it an original love song, that was wrong: it's a
// real cover of Morgan Wallen's 2021 country hit of the same name, which High Watah's own TikTok
// describes as done with "a little island twist." Kyle caught it and flagged the specific twist:
// Wallen's original chorus names a sunburnt Silverado (confirmed via Songfacts, not Tahoe as
// first guessed, but the substance of the correction was right), and High Watah's cover swaps in
// a sunburnt, lifted "Taco'," slang for Tacoma, not the word "Tacoma" itself, a distinction Kyle
// also caught and corrected. Verified directly against Wallen's own lyrics sheet plus the real
// chords/lyrics sheet for High Watah's version on Ultimate Guitar. Described here in paraphrase
// only, not quoted, per the standing rule against reproducing song lyrics.
// Two more real finds, also from Kyle's links, actually resolve the Kalapana question rather
// than just repeat it: Toyota Hawai'i itself sponsored a real 2025 Chinatown pop-up celebrating
// the band Kalapana's 50-year career (a Kalapana box-set reissue by the Honolulu label Aloha Got
// Soul), complete with a "Going Going Gone" collab T-shirt, real, current merch, confirmed
// directly on Aloha Got Soul's own product pages (now sold out). That's a genuine Toyota/Kalapana
// tie the earlier bumper-sticker card didn't have, so it's back, reframed around Toyota's own
// sponsorship rather than Kyle's personal taste. Separately, Servco (Toyota's real Hawai'i
// distributor) released a real, numbered 25-unit "Hawai'i Edition" Tacoma in May 2026, confirmed
// via Servco's own press release plus KHON2 and other real news coverage; Servco's own framing
// (one truck per year the Tacoma has ranked Hawaii's best-selling vehicle, sourced to S&P Global
// Mobility data, 2001-2025) is reported here as Servco's claim, not asserted outright, since an
// earlier pass on this page already found a real complication (a 2018 sales-ranking interruption
// attributed to Jeep Wrangler rental-fleet purchases) that a marketing press release wouldn't
// necessarily surface. That card folds in the Hawaii Auto Outlook pickup-segment numbers too,
// so the standalone Auto Outlook card was retired to avoid repeating the same stat twice.
// Kyle corrected two things about the Kalapana card after it shipped: first, it invented a detail
// that wasn't true, that Kyle didn't already know about the collab before writing this, when he
// actually did, it just isn't as big a deal to him as the card made it sound, so that framing is
// gone. Second, he pointed out that a genuinely bigger, official Toyota collab had already been
// sent (the Zumiez and hufworldwide links) and got skipped over: HUF, a global streetwear brand,
// has real, ongoing, officially licensed partnerships with Toyota Racing Development itself
// ("designed in tandem by HUF and Toyota Racing Division," confirmed directly on both product
// pages), not a local dealer tie-in. That's now its own card.
// The closing "credible impression, not proven" paragraph about a Hawaii lifted-truck scene also
// got a real citation it didn't have before: DrivingLine profiled a named Big Island builder,
// Chris Santos, and his solid-axle-swapped Tacoma, real, if just one documented example, not a
// dataset, of what enthusiasts call "island style," a look that's been around since the 1980s.

const CULTURAL_HIGHLIGHTS = [
	{
		label: "The joke that's actually true",
		body: "Comedian Jo Koy joked Maui is “the world's largest Toyota Tacoma dealership... we got small ones, street customs, ugly ones.” Hawaii News Now checked the bit against real sales data and found Tacomas sell in Hawaii at more than seven times the national average.",
	},
	{
		label: "Toyota's own nod to local music",
		body: "Toyota Hawai'i sponsored a real 2025 Chinatown pop-up celebrating the band Kalapana's 50-year career, tour laminates and family photos on one side, a stage \"loosely inspired by the 70s\" on the other, tied to a real Kalapana box-set reissue by the Honolulu label Aloha Got Soul. It came with a real \"Going Going Gone\" collab T-shirt, now sold out.",
	},
	{
		label: "The bigger version of this is national",
		body: "HUF, a global streetwear brand, has an official, ongoing partnership with Toyota Racing Development itself, not a local dealer tie-in. One shirt, \"designed in tandem by HUF and Toyota Racing Division\" by its own product description, mimics an auto-shop uniform down to fake service-department graphics. Another pulls straight from TRD's own archive to mark the '97 Tacoma. The truck's pull as a lifestyle symbol reaches well past Hawaii and well past a single dealership's marketing budget.",
	},
	{
		label: "Toyota proved the point itself",
		body: "In May 2026, Servco, Toyota's actual Hawai'i distributor, released a numbered, 25-unit \"Hawai'i Edition\" Tacoma, one truck for every year Servco says the Tacoma has ranked the islands' best-selling vehicle, by its own account sourced to S&P Global Mobility's registration data. All 25 sold. It's Toyota's own version of a story Hawaii Auto Outlook's Q1 2025 numbers already told from a different angle: a 38.2% share of the state's pickup segment, more than double the 15.4% national figure.",
	},
	{
		label: "Even the monster truck was a Toyota",
		body: "Poi Pounder debuted in 1985 as Hawaii's first monster truck, and the surviving footage settles any doubt: under the paint and the 66-inch tires is unmistakably a 1984-85 Toyota Pickup cab. It rolled over twice, got rebuilt both times, ran mud bogs and car-crushing shows across the islands, and retired in 1991. A Toyotaholics Facebook post from someone who was apparently there remembers it the same way: \"Back in the early 80's our monster truck was the Poi Pounder... a first gen on the juice!\"",
	},
	{
		label: "A cover song swapped the truck too",
		body: "High Watah's \"Sand In My Boots\" is a real 2025 cover of Morgan Wallen's 2021 country hit, released with what the band itself calls \"a little island twist.\" One of those twists: Wallen's original names a sunburnt Silverado, a Chevy. High Watah's version swaps it for a sunburnt, lifted \"Taco,\" slang for Tacoma, the kind of small, deliberate change that says something about which truck actually reads as home in Hawaii.",
	},
];
---

<section id="hawaii-culture" class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 py-16 md:px-12 md:py-20">
	<h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight md:text-3xl">Toyota, trucks, and Hawaii</h2>
	<p class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
		This truck is a Toyota, and in Hawaii that's never just a brand. It's woven into how the place talks about
		itself, well beyond how many of them get sold.
	</p>

	<div class="mt-8 flex max-w-3xl flex-col gap-6 sm:flex-row sm:items-center">
		<div class="shrink-0 border border-line p-6 text-center sm:w-56">
			<div class="font-display text-5xl font-semibold text-accent-blue">27.6%</div>
			<div class="mt-2 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-soft">of Hawaii's new-vehicle market, 2024</div>
			<div class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink-soft">the single largest share of any brand</div>
		</div>
		<p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
			A number like that, confirmed for 2024 by Hawaii Auto Outlook's own reporting, doesn't happen by
			accident, and it shows up in more than sales charts.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="mt-8 grid max-w-4xl gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
		{
			CULTURAL_HIGHLIGHTS.map((item) => (
				<div class="border border-line p-4">
					<div class="font-display text-sm font-semibold text-ink">{item.label}</div>
					<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">{item.body}</p>
				</div>
			))
		}
	</div>

	<div class="mt-8 max-w-[65ch] space-y-6">
		<p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
			Nationally, the truck and SUV accessory aftermarket, lift kits, wheels, suspension work, the whole
			modified-truck ecosystem, is a real, sizable industry by SEMA's own market data, not a niche hobby.
			Whether it adds up to a genuinely distinct Hawaii scene is still hard to prove with a real dataset, but
			it's not just vendor marketing copy either. Enthusiasts and outlets have a name for it: "island style,"
			solid-axle-swapped, aggressively lifted Toyotas on oversized wheels, a look that's reportedly been around
			since the 1980s. DrivingLine profiled a real, named example: Chris Santos, a Big Island engine-shop owner,
			built a solid-axle-swapped Tacoma with a custom four-link suspension as what he calls his "crown jewel."
			One documented build isn't a census. But it's a real, named example of the thing anyone who's driven
			around Oahu or the Big Island already assumes is true.
		</p>
	</div>
</section>
